  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on prefecture-level macro data to model individual talent mobility decisions, which may obscure finer-grained individual or industry-specific dynamics.",
      "what": "Using data from prefecture-level cities in China spanning 2010 to 2022, this study constructed a gravity model and applied a double machine learning approach to evaluate how smart city pilot policies affect technical talent mobility. The analysis reveals that smart city designation increases technical talent mobility by an average of 1.910% overall, with more pronounced increases of 5.489% in provincial capitals and 4.856% in eastern region cities.",
      "why": "It quantifies how smart city policies redistribute technical labor, demonstrating that while digital growth and internationalization act as pull factors, they simultaneously trigger push factors like digital-technology-related unemployment and heightened market competition."
    }
